Karen Stein and John Hodges have been involved in the project almost 
from the earliest years.  John Hodges predates the split with TLI 
Loglan, and he recruited Karen and several others in the Virginia Tech 
(university) community near Blacksburg VA (about 6 hours drive from 
here), in the early to mid 90s.  John and Karen and occasionally others 
from their group were present at nearly every LogFest held at my house 
here in the Washington DC area until around 2002.  Except for a few 
people around here, they are the oldest "group" of Lojban students in 
existence.  There were other groups, in LA, SF, Boston and Yorkshire 
England that formed shortly thereafter, but I don't know that any of 
them still think of themselves as a "group" like the Blacksburg people 
apparently do.

I am sure that John Clifford remembers Karen from LogFests, since they 
were occasionally at odds %^)

The DC area group largely fell apart when Athelstan, one of its leaders, 
suffered brain trauma in an auto accident in 1992 and never recovered 
enough to resume leadership, though a few people showed up at LogFest 
when we held them here - not so much as a "group" though.

The LA area group was centered on Jim Carter, who also invented guaspi. 
  I've heard from none of them since 1994 (and undoubtedly the failure 
to continue publishing Ju'i Lobypli was even more important than the 
delayed baseline in keeping these off-line communities together and 
involved.

